WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.899
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:01.899 --> 00:00:03.899
Alice and Bob are on a game show.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:07.889
Each is secretly told a whole positive number.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:13.949
They are told the two numbers are consecutive, but neither knows the other person's number.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:22.469
For example, if Alice is told 20 she does not know if Bob was told 19 or 21.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:28.769
And if Bob is told 21 he does not know if Alice was told 20 or 22.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:36.120
The point of the game is to guess the other person's number, but there are rules to the game.

00:00:37.149 --> 00:00:42.389
Alice and Bob cannot communicate with each other and they are not allowed to plan a strategy either.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.159
The two are in a room where a clock rings every minute.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:54.960
After the clock rings either player can call out a guess of the other player's number or they can both stay silent.

00:00:56.199 --> 00:01:00.029
Here's a clock that rings every minute on the minute.

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:07.290
The game continues until Alice or Bob makes a guess.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.830
After the first guest is made the game ends.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:19.500
Alice and Bob win 1 million dollars if the guess is correct and they lose and get nothing if the guess is incorrect

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.659
How can Alice and Bob best play this game to win?

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.559
Each knows the other is perfect at logical reasoning.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:35.250
Can you figure it out? Give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching the video for the solution.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:45.089
At first it seems like Alice and Bob can do no better than random chance.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:51.659
If Alice is told 20 for instance there is no way to know if Bob has 19 or 21.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:59.399
But since Alice can limit Bob's number to two possibilities, she can at least have a 50% chance of guessing correctly.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.409
Bob has the same issue: if he was told the number n,

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:12.159
then he cannot be sure if Alice was told n minus 1 or n plus 1. If Bob guesses between two possibilities

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.669
then he also has a 50% chance of guessing correctly.

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:23.019
Acting individually Alice and Bob have a 50% chance of guessing the other person's number and winning the game.

00:02:24.799 --> 00:02:29.139
Remarkably they can do much better. There is a method they can win

00:02:29.989 --> 00:02:33.548
100% of the time if they use logic perfectly.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:38.709
One key detail is the two are given positive

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:47.440
consecutive numbers. When Alice gets the number n, she usually has to consider if Bob has n minus 1 or n plus 1.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.529
But suppose Alice gets 1.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.060
Bob could have 0 or 2

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:02.520
But 0 is not a positive number so Bob must have 2.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.820
(Search your feelings Alice, you know it to be 2.)

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.780
A player that gets 1 will know the other person has 2 and

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:16.869
since the player is sure the player will guess on the very first ring on the clock,

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:24.370
they will win 1 million dollars for sure.

00:03:25.489 --> 00:03:27.340
This was from Alice's perspective

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:32.560
But of course if Bob got 1 he would also figure out that Alice has 2 on the first ring of the clock.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:41.410
But what about larger numbers? We can continue the logic. Suppose Alice gets two and the first ring of the clock passes.

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:45.069
Bob could have 1 or 3.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:50.199
But if Bob had 1 he would have guessed on the first ring of the clock.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.719
So Bob does not guess after one ring then Bob must have 3.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:02.169
Alice realizes this after the first ring and she will guess on the second ring of the clock.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:09.160
In this way Alice and Bob will win 1 million dollars for sure.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:17.229
The logic would be similar if Bob got 2 and the first ring of the clock passes he would know that Alice had 3.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:23.529
So now what about larger numbers? We can continue the logic inductively.

00:04:24.439 --> 00:04:29.859
Suppose Alice gets n, and the clock rings n minus one times with no guess from Bob.

00:04:32.509 --> 00:04:37.658
If Bob had n minus one he would have guessed on the n minus one ring of the clock.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:44.169
Since Bob did not guess then Bob must have had the larger number n plus one.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:50.829
Alice therefore guesses that Bob has n plus one after the clock rings exactly n times.

00:04:54.349 --> 00:04:58.059
They will win the game for sure and get 1 million dollars.

00:04:59.629 --> 00:05:02.199
Bob would of course reason similarly.

00:05:04.219 --> 00:05:08.199
So in summary Alice and Bob can always win the game

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.459
when they're given two numbers n and n plus 1.

00:05:13.219 --> 00:05:15.549
The person who gets the smaller number n

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:21.789
will always guess after the clock ring exactly n times that the other player has n plus 1.

00:05:22.099 --> 00:05:25.028
This will be correct, and they will always win the game.

